// A single-argument top-level `let rec` whose recursive call is in tail position
// must compile to a LOOP, not to recursion.
//
// Why it needs pinning. The C backend's direct N-ary form began as a calling
// convention -- uncurrying a multi-argument top-level fn -- and was gated on
// `params >= 2`, since one parameter has nothing to uncurry. The tail-call loop
// transform (Q-029) is reachable ONLY through that form, so the gate silently
// excluded every one-argument top-level `let rec` from it. Such functions still
// RAN, because clang's sibling-call optimisation flattened them, and that is a
// property of the optimiser rather than of the program: memu's RISC-V emulator
// is `let rec drive = fn n -> ... drive (n + 1)`, so its recursion depth is the
// emulated instruction count, and adding a syscall service to the same file was
// enough to change clang's mind. Every long-running guest then died with the
// emulator's own "stack overflow (recursion too deep)", reported against every
// program in the RV corpus at once -- which reads exactly like a regression in
// the compiler under test.
//
// THIS FILE IS THE RUNTIME HALF, AND IT IS THE WEAKER HALF. At -O2 clang
// flattens the recursion anyway, so no iteration count proves the transform is
// present on the C backend; and the counts here are bounded by the INTERPRETER,
// which has no such transform and which the parity harness requires to succeed.
// The real pin is on the emitted code -- three assertions in test/test_basic.ml
// that the C carries `goto __mere_tail` -- because the optimiser must not be the
// thing that decides whether the language keeps its promise. What this file adds
// is the other three backends at runtime, and a shape for each of the two
// aliasing cases below.
let rec count_down = fn (n: int) -> if n <= 0 then 0 else count_down (n - 1);
let _ = print_int (count_down 200000);

// The accumulating shape too, since it is the one that carries a value through
// and therefore the one whose parameter aliasing can go wrong: a `let` in the
// body that shadows the parameter must not be what the jump writes.
// The carried value COUNTS rather than sums: `acc + n` over 200000 terms is
// 20,000,100,000, which does not fit this project's 32-bit target and made the
// RV32 column answer -1474736480 -- a width difference dressed up as a
// tail-call failure. The loop is what this file is about, so the accumulator
// has no business being wide.
let rec sum_to = fn (n: int) -> fn (acc: int) ->
  if n <= 0 then acc else sum_to (n - 1) (acc + 1);
let _ = print_int (sum_to 200000 0);

// ...and a one-argument shape whose count the optimiser cannot see, so it
// cannot collapse the loop to a closed form and pass without looping at all.
// `str_len` of a literal is opaque enough and needs no host.
let seed = str_len "0123456789" * 20000;
let rec walk = fn (n: int) -> if n <= 0 then 7 else walk (n - 1);
let _ = print_int (walk seed);
